<body>
  <GameNav />
  <nav class="breadcrumb" aria-label="Breadcrumb"><a href="/">Home</a> &gt; <a href="/rooms-and-exits/">Rooms &amp; Exits</a> &gt; Chapter 3 &gt; Level 13</nav>

  <main class="seo-section">
    <h1>Rooms &amp; Exits Chapter 3 Level 13 Walkthrough: Kids Bedroom 1</h1>

    <p>Kids Bedroom 1 is the thirteenth room of Chapter 3, <i>Murder Dressed in White</i>, and it is the opposite of Kitchen in almost every way. Kitchen was one room with one long chain of locks; this is a small pastel bedroom packed with nine or ten separate toys, each of which is its own little puzzle. There is no keypad on the wall and no single code to type at the door. What the level actually hands you is a wooden box with two numbers on its sides, a working calculator on the desk, and a matchstick display built into the box lid.</p>

    <div class="disclaimer">Unofficial fan-made guide. Not affiliated with Webelinx Games, the developer of Rooms &amp; Exits: Escape Room Games.</div>

    <div class="verify-note">Rebuilt frame by frame at one frame per second. The numbers <b>74</b> and <b>4</b> on the wooden box were each read twice — once from the whole frame at 1:24 and once from a 2x crop of the box — and both passes agreed. The calculator result <b>17760</b> was read twice from the LCD itself, at two separate seconds, and cross-checked against the arithmetic. Anything that only ever read one way, or that two passes read differently, is listed under "What this page could not verify" instead of being printed as a code.</div>

    <LevelSelector chapter={3} current={13} variant="jump" label="Chapter 3 · Murder Dressed in White" />

    <div class="answer-box">
      <span class="label">Quick answer</span>
      <p>Kids Bedroom 1 is room 13 of 15 in Chapter 3. The wooden box carries <b>74</b> (front panel) and <b>4</b> (side panel); the desk calculator turns <b>48 × 5</b> into <b>240</b> and then <b>240 × 74</b> into <b>17,760</b>.</p>
      <ol>
        <li>Start with the tablet on the rug: it asks you to <b>Fill the white squares</b> with the orange blocks. No digits involved.</li>
        <li>Inspect the wooden box. Front green panel <b>74</b>, right green panel <b>4</b>. Its lid is a five-cell segment display with matchsticks, dark until you have the matches.</li>
        <li>Work the toy track on the rug, the sliding smiley-block board (the long one), the fish tangram, the six animal knobs and the butterfly pattern picture.</li>
        <li>Open the desk drawer with the <b>key</b> already in its lock, and pick up the <b>Matches</b>.</li>
        <li>At the desk calculator, run <b>48 × 5 = 240</b>, then <b>240 × 74 = 17,760</b> — the five-digit result is what the level is building toward.</li>
        <li>Set the box-lid matchstick display. This is where our recording stops, so the exact digit pattern is not printed here.</li>
      </ol>
    </div>

    <div class="video-wrap">
      <div class="yt-facade" data-yt="VMJIdMH_xxI" data-title="Rooms &amp; Exits Level 13 Kids Bedroom 1 Walkthrough Murder Dressed in White Chapter 3"
           role="button" tabindex="0" aria-label="Play video: Rooms &amp; Exits Level 13 Kids Bedroom 1 Walkthrough">
        <img src="https://i.ytimg.com/vi/VMJIdMH_xxI/maxresdefault.jpg"
             alt="Rooms and Exits Chapter 3 Level 13 Kids Bedroom 1 walkthrough video" loading="lazy" decoding="async" width="1280" height="720"
             onerror="this.onerror=null;this.src='https://i.ytimg.com/vi/VMJIdMH_xxI/hqdefault.jpg'">
      </div>
    </div>
    <p class="video-cap">The Kids Bedroom 1 run used for every frame on this page.</p>

    <img class="hero" src="/rooms-and-exits/c3l13-hero-bedroom.jpg" alt="Rooms and Exits Chapter 3 Level 13 Kids Bedroom 1: a pastel bedroom with blue and white stripes on the left wall, pink and white stripes on the right, a white bed with pink bedding, a white wardrobe, and a desk under a window with a bright green chair" width="1200" height="594" />

    <h2>What this level actually is<span class="accent">.</span></h2>
    <p>One square bedroom, seen from a high angle, with three walls in play. The left wall is striped blue and white and carries a plain white door, a fabric toy bin printed with the alphabet, and a wooden easel holding a painting of a butterfly tree. The back wall is a wide window with pink curtains, a desk underneath it, and a lime-green chair. The right wall is striped pink and white, and against it sit the wardrobe and the bed — white frame, pink duvet, a green gift box sitting on the mattress.</p>
    <p>The floor is the busiest surface in the room. A large pale rug covers most of it, and on the rug are a pink pouf, a blue star cushion, a segmented caterpillar toy curled into a question mark, and a red-yellow-green toy track that the game turns into its own puzzle. Two red and blue high-top sneakers sit by the bed. Almost every object in this room is interactive, which is why the recording spends the first minute simply opening things.</p>
    <p>Structurally, this is a hub level rather than a chain. Kitchen made you solve one lock to reach the next; Kids Bedroom 1 lets you work its puzzles in almost any order and only links them at the end, through the box: the box's side numbers feed the calculator, the calculator's result feeds the box's lid display, and the matches you pick up are what let you set that display. Because the parts are independent, you can leave the sliding-block board for last without blocking anything.</p>

    <h2>The map: what is where<span class="accent">.</span></h2>
    <p>Everything we could identify, and what it is for.</p>
    <table class="item-table">
      <thead><tr><th>Object</th><th>Where</th><th>What it does</th></tr></thead>
      <tbody>
        <tr><td>Tablet</td><td>On the rug, centre left</td><td>Grid puzzle with the instruction <b>FILL THE WHITE SQUARES</b>. Orange stud-topped blocks into white cells.</td></tr>
        <tr><td>Wooden box</td><td>Top shelf of the wardrobe; also a full inspect view</td><td>Green side panels printed <b>74</b> (front) and <b>4</b> (right). Lid is a five-cell matchstick display.</td></tr>
        <tr><td>Matchstick display</td><td>Built into the box lid</td><td>Five seven-segment cells made of matches, one diagonal match out of place.</td></tr>
        <tr><td>Calculator</td><td>On the desk under the window</td><td>Working calculator; the run uses <b>48 × 5 = 240</b> then <b>240 × 74 = 17,760</b>.</td></tr>
        <tr><td>Toy track</td><td>Rug, in front of the bed</td><td>Red, yellow and green track segments with puzzle-piece connectors; join the round ends.</td></tr>
        <tr><td>Sliding smiley blocks</td><td>Full-screen board</td><td>Coloured square blocks with faces; the level's longest puzzle, about 2:06 to 3:36.</td></tr>
        <tr><td>Fish tangram</td><td>Framed close-up on a textured panel</td><td>Dark fish silhouette filled with red and blue triangles.</td></tr>
        <tr><td>Animal-knob dresser</td><td>Under the desk, right side</td><td>Six knobs: frog, crocodile, hippo, lion, duck, mushroom.</td></tr>
        <tr><td>Butterfly picture</td><td>Framed, and again on the easel</td><td>Carries the rule: <b>count the number of butterfly patterns that have the same color</b>.</td></tr>
        <tr><td>Desk drawer + key</td><td>Under the desk</td><td>Silver <b>key</b> already in the top drawer's keyhole at 5:00.</td></tr>
        <tr><td>Matches</td><td>Inventory pickup</td><td>Labelled <b>Matches</b>; brown box, two rows of gold-headed matches.</td></tr>
        <tr><td>Switch chest</td><td>Full-screen close-up</td><td>Four colour modules (red, yellow, green, purple) of four toggle switches each — sixteen switches.</td></tr>
        <tr><td>Wardrobe</td><td>Right wall, beside the window</td><td>Opens to hanging clothes, folded stacks and a dark green <b>backpack</b>.</td></tr>
        <tr><td>Green gift box</td><td>On the bed</td><td>Green box with a pink ribbon; sparkles when examined.</td></tr>
      </tbody>
    </table>

    <h2>Step by step<span class="accent">.</span></h2>
    <p>The order below follows the recording. Timestamps are video time; the links jump to the matching second.</p>

    <div class="step-card">
      <span class="step-num">Step 1 · 0:00</span>
      <strong>Read the room before you touch it</strong>
      The level opens on a title card — <b>Level 13 / Kids Bedroom 1</b> — over the full room, then the camera settles and the HUD appears. Sweep it once: door, toy bin and easel left; window, desk and green chair centre; wardrobe and bed right. Almost everything down at floor level on the rug is a puzzle, so resist the urge to start tapping the furniture first.
      <img src="/rooms-and-exits/c3l13-level-title.jpg" alt="The Level 13 Kids Bedroom 1 title card in Rooms and Exits Chapter 3" width="1200" height="594" loading="lazy" />
      <div class="ts">Watch: <a href="https://www.youtube.com/watch?v=VMJIdMH_xxI&t=0s" target="_blank" rel="noopener">0:00</a></div>
    </div>

    <div class="step-card">
      <span class="step-num">Step 2 · 0:06 · the tablet</span>
      <strong>"Fill the white squares"</strong>
      The tablet on the rug opens first, and it is the only puzzle in the room that states its own rule in words: <b>FILL THE WHITE SQUARES</b>. The board is a purple patterned grid with scattered white cells, and the pieces are orange blocks with a yellow stud on top. You are filling the gaps, not matching a picture, so work from the edges inward. The recording clears it in about thirty-five seconds.
      <img src="/rooms-and-exits/c3l13-tablet-fill-white-squares.jpg" alt="The tablet puzzle in Kids Bedroom 1 with the instruction Fill the white squares, a purple grid and orange blocks" width="1200" height="594" loading="lazy" />
      <div class="ts">Watch: <a href="https://www.youtube.com/watch?v=VMJIdMH_xxI&t=6s" target="_blank" rel="noopener">0:06</a></div>
    </div>

    <div class="step-card">
      <span class="step-num">Step 3 · 1:24 · the numbers that matter</span>
      <strong>A wooden box printed 74 and 4</strong>
      The game pulls the wooden box up as a full inspect view, and this is the single most useful frame in the level. It is light wood with bright green side panels, each crossed by an X-shaped wooden brace, and the numbers are printed in pale green: <b>74</b> across the front panel, <b>4</b> on the right-hand panel. The lid holds a dark five-cell segment display with no lit segments at all. Remember where this box lives — at 5:24 you can see it on the wardrobe's top shelf. A <b>Piece of paper</b> with a fox and a bear drawn on it comes out of the same area just before this, at 1:06.
      <img src="/rooms-and-exits/c3l13-wooden-box-74.jpg" alt="The wooden box in Kids Bedroom 1 with green side panels printed 74 on the front and 4 on the right" width="1200" height="594" loading="lazy" />
      <div class="ts">Watch: <a href="https://www.youtube.com/watch?v=VMJIdMH_xxI&t=84s" target="_blank" rel="noopener">1:24</a></div>
    </div>

    <div class="step-card">
      <span class="step-num">Step 4 · 1:36 · the track</span>
      <strong>Join the toy track on the rug</strong>
      The red-yellow-green track on the rug opens as its own board: curved and straight segments with puzzle-piece tabs, and a round end cap at each side. The goal is one continuous run from the green cap to the orange cap. It is a rotation-and-fit puzzle, so the tabs tell you which way a piece has to face before you worry about its colour.
      <img src="/rooms-and-exits/c3l13-track-puzzle.jpg" alt="The toy track puzzle in Kids Bedroom 1, red yellow and green segments with puzzle-piece connectors" width="1200" height="594" loading="lazy" />
      <div class="ts">Watch: <a href="https://www.youtube.com/watch?v=VMJIdMH_xxI&t=96s" target="_blank" rel="noopener">1:36</a></div>
    </div>

    <div class="step-card">
      <span class="step-num">Step 5 · 2:06 to 3:36 · the long one</span>
      <strong>The sliding-block board of smiley faces</strong>
      This is the level's time sink. A full-screen frame of coloured square blocks, each with a face, shuffled around one gap. The recording grinds on it for a minute and a half with the room clock running, which is the honest signal for how fiddly it is. We are not printing a move list: the footage is cut and the block layout changes between sampled seconds, so a guessed sequence would cost you more time than working it out yourself.
      <img src="/rooms-and-exits/c3l13-sliding-smiley-blocks.jpg" alt="The sliding block puzzle in Kids Bedroom 1 with coloured square blocks carrying smiley faces" width="1200" height="594" loading="lazy" />
      <div class="ts">Watch: <a href="https://www.youtube.com/watch?v=VMJIdMH_xxI&t=126s" target="_blank" rel="noopener">2:06</a> · <a href="https://www.youtube.com/watch?v=VMJIdMH_xxI&t=216s" target="_blank" rel="noopener">3:36</a></div>
    </div>

    <div class="step-card">
      <span class="step-num">Step 6 · 3:42 · the tangram</span>
      <strong>Fill the fish silhouette</strong>
      A framed close-up on a textured grey panel, with a dark fish shape left as a silhouette. The pieces you place are right-angled triangles — in the frames we sampled, one dark red and one pale blue. It is a tangram in everything but name: cover the outline exactly, and the triangle count is the constraint, not the colours.
      <img src="/rooms-and-exits/c3l13-fish-tangram.jpg" alt="The fish tangram puzzle in Kids Bedroom 1 with red and blue triangles over a fish silhouette" width="1200" height="594" loading="lazy" />
      <div class="ts">Watch: <a href="https://www.youtube.com/watch?v=VMJIdMH_xxI&t=222s" target="_blank" rel="noopener">3:42</a></div>
    </div>

    <div class="step-card">
      <span class="step-num">Step 7 · 4:06 · the dresser</span>
      <strong>Six animal knobs, three drawers</strong>
      The dresser under the desk has three drawers and six round knobs, each printed with a creature: <b>green frog</b> and <b>green crocodile</b> on the top drawer, <b>white hippo</b> and <b>yellow lion</b> on the middle, <b>yellow duck</b> and <b>yellow mushroom</b> on the bottom. The top pair is tied together by a dark cord with a knot in the middle. Treat it as a state puzzle: what matters is which knob you turn and in what order, and the pairing on each drawer is the hint.
      <img src="/rooms-and-exits/c3l13-animal-knobs.jpg" alt="The three-drawer dresser in Kids Bedroom 1 with six animal knobs: frog, crocodile, hippo, lion, duck and mushroom" width="1200" height="594" loading="lazy" />
      <div class="ts">Watch: <a href="https://www.youtube.com/watch?v=VMJIdMH_xxI&t=246s" target="_blank" rel="noopener">4:06</a></div>
    </div>

    <div class="step-card">
      <span class="step-num">Step 8 · 4:18 · the butterfly rule</span>
      <strong>"Don't count how many butterflies there are"</strong>
      A framed picture of a tree covered in butterflies, and under it the level's most explicit instruction: <b>DON'T COUNT HOW MANY BUTTERFLIES THERE ARE! COUNT THE NUMBER OF BUTTERFLY PATTERNS THAT HAVE THE SAME COLOR</b>. Read it literally. The trick is that the butterflies are all different — you are counting how many share one colour scheme and wing pattern, not how many insects are on the tree. The easel on the left of the room holds the same butterfly tree, so the two are meant to be read together.
      <img src="/rooms-and-exits/c3l13-butterfly-counting.jpg" alt="The butterfly tree picture in Kids Bedroom 1 with the instruction not to count how many butterflies there are but to count butterfly patterns that have the same color" width="1200" height="594" loading="lazy" />
      <div class="ts">Watch: <a href="https://www.youtube.com/watch?v=VMJIdMH_xxI&t=258s" target="_blank" rel="noopener">4:18</a></div>
    </div>

    <div class="step-card">
      <span class="step-num">Step 9 · 5:00 · the key and the matches</span>
      <strong>Turn the key, take the matches</strong>
      The desk drawer close-up shows a small silver <b>key</b> already sitting in the keyhole of the white top drawer, with the gold knob right beside it — you turn it rather than hunt for it. A few seconds later the <b>Matches</b> come into the inventory: a brown matchbox with the tray slid out and two neat rows of gold-headed matches, labelled simply <b>Matches</b>. Those matches are the tool for the box lid. Right after, the wardrobe opens at 5:24 — hanging clothes, folded stacks, and a dark green backpack on the middle shelf, with the wooden box from step 3 sitting on the top shelf.
      <img src="/rooms-and-exits/c3l13-matches-item.jpg" alt="The Matches item in Kids Bedroom 1, a brown matchbox with two rows of gold-headed matches" width="1200" height="594" loading="lazy" />
      <img src="/rooms-and-exits/c3l13-wardrobe-backpack.jpg" alt="The open wardrobe in Kids Bedroom 1 with hanging clothes, a green backpack and the wooden box on the top shelf" width="1200" height="594" loading="lazy" />
      <div class="ts">Watch: <a href="https://www.youtube.com/watch?v=VMJIdMH_xxI&t=300s" target="_blank" rel="noopener">5:00</a> · <a href="https://www.youtube.com/watch?v=VMJIdMH_xxI&t=318s" target="_blank" rel="noopener">5:18</a> · <a href="https://www.youtube.com/watch?v=VMJIdMH_xxI&t=324s" target="_blank" rel="noopener">5:24</a></div>
    </div>

    <div class="step-card">
      <span class="step-num">Step 10 · 6:00 · the switch chest</span>
      <strong>Four colours, sixteen switches</strong>
      A wooden chest with a brass latch opens as a close-up, and under the latch is the panel: <b>four coloured modules — red, yellow, green, purple — each holding four brass toggle switches</b>. Sixteen switches, all down when you find it. This is a state puzzle, not a code entry: the colours group the switches, and whatever pattern the room has been showing you is what you have to reproduce across the four groups.
      <img src="/rooms-and-exits/c3l13-switch-chest.jpg" alt="The wooden chest in Kids Bedroom 1 with a brass latch and a panel of red, yellow, green and purple modules each holding four toggle switches" width="1200" height="594" loading="lazy" />
      <div class="ts">Watch: <a href="https://www.youtube.com/watch?v=VMJIdMH_xxI&t=360s" target="_blank" rel="noopener">6:00</a></div>
    </div>

    <div class="step-card">
      <span class="step-num">Step 11 · 6:45 · the calculator</span>
      <strong>48 × 5 = 240, then 240 × 74 = 17,760</strong>
      The calculator on the desk is not scenery — it is the level's arithmetic station, and the run uses it in two steps. At 6:45 the LCD reads <b>48*5</b>; a second later it reads <b>240*</b>; at 6:47 and again at 6:48 it reads <b>17760</b>. So the chain is 48 × 5 = 240, then 240 × 74 = 17,760, with the 74 coming straight off the wooden box's front panel. The result sits on the LCD as five plain digits with no decimal point, which is exactly the width of the box-lid display.
      <img src="/rooms-and-exits/c3l13-calculator-17760.jpg" alt="The desk calculator in Kids Bedroom 1 with its LCD reading 17760" width="1200" height="594" loading="lazy" />
      <div class="ts">Watch: <a href="https://www.youtube.com/watch?v=VMJIdMH_xxI&t=405s" target="_blank" rel="noopener">6:45</a> · <a href="https://www.youtube.com/watch?v=VMJIdMH_xxI&t=407s" target="_blank" rel="noopener">6:47</a></div>
    </div>

    <div class="step-card">
      <span class="step-num">Step 12 · 7:00 to 7:12 · where the video stops</span>
      <strong>The matchstick display on the box lid</strong>
      The box's lid comes back as a full-screen board: five seven-segment cells with the segments made of individual matchsticks, burnt heads and all, and one cell that always carries a <b>diagonal matchstick</b> sticking out of the digit. That diagonal is the tell — the game is showing you a match that has been moved out of position, and the puzzle is about moving matches until the cells read what they are supposed to read. The cells genuinely change from second to second as the player works. Then the recording ends.
      <img src="/rooms-and-exits/c3l13-matchstick-display.jpg" alt="The matchstick segment display on the wooden box lid in Kids Bedroom 1, five cells with a diagonal matchstick" width="1200" height="594" loading="lazy" />
      <div class="ts">Watch: <a href="https://www.youtube.com/watch?v=VMJIdMH_xxI&t=420s" target="_blank" rel="noopener">7:00</a></div>
    </div>

    <h2>Where people get stuck<span class="accent">.</span></h2>
    <p><b>You keep looking for a keypad.</b> There isn't one, and that is deliberate. Kids Bedroom 1 does its locking with objects — a box lid, a switch panel, a dresser of knobs — rather than with a number pad on a wall. The only digits the room actually asks you to produce come out of the calculator.</p>
    <p><b>You read 74 and 4 as a single code.</b> They are not a two-digit and a one-digit code sitting next to each other; they are two numbers on two different faces of the same box, and the level expects you to do something with them. The run we worked from multiplies: 48 × 5 first, then 240 × 74, ending on 17,760.</p>
    <p><b>The box lid is dark and looks broken.</b> It is supposed to be. The five-cell display is unlit when you first inspect the box at 1:24 and only becomes a puzzle once you have the <b>Matches</b> from 5:18. If you found the box early and thought it was decoration, go back to it after the drawer.</p>
    <p><b>The butterfly picture feels like a trick question.</b> It is, but the wording is precise: <b>count the number of butterfly patterns that have the same color</b>. Counting the insects gets you a number that is too big and always wrong. Group by colour scheme first, then count the group.</p>
    <p><b>The sliding-block board eats your time.</b> Plan for it. It is the one puzzle in this room that the recording spends more than a minute on, and there is no hint that solves it for you. Leave it until the quick pickups are done so you are not losing progress elsewhere while you grind it.</p>

    <h2>Where Kids Bedroom 1 sits in Chapter 3<span class="accent">.</span></h2>
    <p>Chapter 3 is <i>Murder Dressed in White</i>, and its fifteen rooms run from the Dining Room through the house and out into the garden: Dining Room, Living Room, Study Room, Guest Toilet, Workshop, Guest Bedroom, Master Bathroom, Kids Playroom, Backyard, Garden, Kitchen, Driveway, <b>Kids Bedroom</b>, Storage Room, Garage. Kids Bedroom is room <b>13 of 15</b> — the third-from-last room in the chapter, straight after the Driveway and before the Storage Room.</p>
    <p>Mechanically it is a reset after Kitchen. Kitchen was a chain of dependencies in one big room; this is a small room where most puzzles stand alone, and the only chain is the one that ends at the box: numbers on the box, calculator on the desk, matches from the drawer, display in the lid. That makes it a good room to clear in one pass — grab the pickups first, then settle in for the sliding blocks.</p>

